            <p>
                The illustrative table omits ratios because a single baseline would mix different operations
                and payload sizes. Compare matching serializer, operation, and payload pairs; verify correctness
                with contract tests outside the benchmark. Measurements inform a decision but do not guarantee
                production performance.
            </p>

            <h4>Keep Performance Checks Separate From Unit-Test Assertions</h4>
            <p>
                Avoid enforcing strict elapsed-time ratios with <Highlight>Stopwatch</Highlight> inside an
                ordinary xUnit test. JIT compilation, garbage collection, machine load, and shared CI
                resources can make such a test unstable. The following illustrates a brittle approach,
                not a recommended performance gate:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Anti-pattern: wall-clock assertions are sensitive to runtime and CI conditions.
// Prefer controlled benchmarks and investigate meaningful changes in context.
// Tests/InvoiceSerializerRollbackCriteriaTests.cs
using System.Diagnostics;
using InvoiceQueue.Application;
using InvoiceQueue.Domain;
using InvoiceQueue.Infrastructure;
using Xunit;

namespace InvoiceQueue.Tests;

public sealed class InvoiceSerializerRollbackCriteriaTests
{
    // Accepted performance degradation tolerance.
    // 1.10 means the candidate is allowed to be at most 10 % slower than the baseline.
    // Use your BenchmarkDotNet results to choose a defensible value.
    private const double MaxSlowdownRatio = 1.10;

    private const int WarmUpRuns = 200;
    private const int MeasuredRuns = 10_000;

    // A representative invoice that exercises the full serializer path:
    // decimal, DateTimeOffset, enum, string, nested list.
    private static readonly Invoice RepresentativeInvoice = new(
        Id: 1,
        CustomerName: "Rollback Gate Corp",
        Amount: 4_999.99m,
        IssuedAt: new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero),
        Status: InvoiceStatus.Sent,
        Notes: "Performance gate fixture",
        LineItems:
        [
            new InvoiceLineItem("Consulting", 5, 500.00m),
            new InvoiceLineItem("Monthly Hosting", 12, 99.99m),
            new InvoiceLineItem("Priority Support", 1, 2_400.00m)
        ]);

    [Fact]
    public void Modern_serializer_serialize_is_not_slower_than_legacy_by_more_than_10_percent()
    {
        var legacy = new LegacyJsonInvoiceSerializer();
        var modern = new SystemTextJsonInvoiceSerializer();

        var baselineMs = MeasureMs(() =&gt; legacy.Serialize(RepresentativeInvoice));
        var candidateMs = MeasureMs(() =&gt; modern.Serialize(RepresentativeInvoice));

        var ratio = candidateMs / baselineMs;
        Assert.True(
            ratio &lt;= MaxSlowdownRatio,
            $"Modern serializer (serialize) is {ratio:P0} of legacy baseline. " +
            $"Candidate: {candidateMs:F2} ms  |  Baseline: {baselineMs:F2} ms. " +
            $"Limit: {MaxSlowdownRatio:P0}. Roll back this replacement.");
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Modern_serializer_deserialize_is_not_slower_than_legacy_by_more_than_10_percent()
    {
        var legacy = new LegacyJsonInvoiceSerializer();
        var modern = new SystemTextJsonInvoiceSerializer();
        var json = legacy.Serialize(RepresentativeInvoice);

        var baselineMs = MeasureMs(() =&gt; legacy.Deserialize(json));
        var candidateMs = MeasureMs(() =&gt; modern.Deserialize(json));

        var ratio = candidateMs / baselineMs;
        Assert.True(
            ratio &lt;= MaxSlowdownRatio,
            $"Modern serializer (deserialize) is {ratio:P0} of legacy baseline. " +
            $"Candidate: {candidateMs:F2} ms  |  Baseline: {baselineMs:F2} ms. " +
            $"Limit: {MaxSlowdownRatio:P0}. Roll back this replacement.");
    }

    private static double MeasureMs(Action operation)
    {
        // Burn through JIT compilation and initial allocations.
        for (var i = 0; i &lt; WarmUpRuns; i++) operation();

        var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        for (var i = 0; i &lt; MeasuredRuns; i++) operation();
        stopwatch.Stop();

        return stopwatch.Elapsed.TotalMilliseconds;
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Use Each Check for the Question It Can Answer</h4>
            <p>
                Each check covers a different part of a replacement. A passing result is limited to the
                assertions, fixtures, and measurement environment it used:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
  ┌───────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Check             │ What a pass indicates                                   │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Snapshot tests    │ Selected outputs match accepted snapshots               │
  │ Contract tests    │ Current assertions pass for the tested cases            │
  │ Integration tests │ Selected consumers work with the tested implementation │
  │ Benchmarks        │ Measurements for the workload and environment tested    │
  └───────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Contract tests should run in CI when a change touches:
  - The interface definition
  - Any implementation of the interface
  - The DI registration that selects the implementation
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Use CI failures to review whether tested requirements still hold.
                Benchmark results complement correctness tests; neither a successful benchmark nor a green
                test suite proves behavior for every input or production condition.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                Contract tests make selected interface requirements executable and reusable across
                implementations. A passing suite is evidence for its tested cases, not proof of equivalence
                across all possible inputs. Key points:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Characterization tests</Highlight> compare selected outputs with accepted
                    snapshots. Review snapshots against the intended format and retain useful cases as
                    regression coverage.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <Highlight>Contract tests</Highlight> encode specified behaviors (round-trip
                    correctness, null handling, enum fidelity, Unicode, empty collections) in an
                    abstract base class and run them against the implementations you register. Add cases
                    for important wire-format requirements and downstream behavior as needed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Use an abstract base class with one <Highlight>[Fact]</Highlight> per behavioral
                    obligation and one concrete sub-class per implementation. The test runner discovers
                    and executes all subclasses automatically.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Use <Highlight>BenchmarkDotNet</Highlight> in Release configuration for controlled
                    comparisons of representative workloads; results vary by environment.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Avoid strict timing assertions in ordinary xUnit tests. Shared CI machines and runtime
                    conditions can make elapsed-time thresholds unreliable.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A green contract suite means its current assertions pass for the tested cases. Pair it
                    with integration tests, NuGet Audit, and review of the resolved package graph before
                    completing a dependency replacement.
                </li>
            </ul>
